ired structured fields\n- Unit tests asserting field presence and correctness via test logger output, per controller tier and across all 15 action files\n- Backwards-compatibility validation: before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error; verify a log consumer (Loki, CloudWatch) ingests both formats without configuration changes; coordinate with SRE/Support to identify and update text-based alert rules that rely on message-string patterns (pre-merge dev-cluster validation)\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given a reconciliation error log call in any manager-binary controller that previously embedded resource name or namespace in the message string, when the code is updated, then the log call uses structured key-value pairs (name, namespace) instead — measured by the CI lint rule/static analysis flagging zero remaining log.Error calls with name/namespace embedded in message text in this binary\n- Given the DSCI controller's reconciliation error logs, when the code is updated, then all structured field names use the camelCase convention (name, namespace, resourceKind) — measured by unit test asserting DSCI error logs contain the standard field names and a codebase grep confirming no references to Request.Name/DSCInitialization field names remain\n- Given a service controller (Gateway, Auth, Monitoring, CertConfigMapGenerator, Setup) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the log entry includes resourceKind with the correct value — measured by unit test parsing test logger output\n- Given an action file error log across all 4 tiers (15 files), when the code is updated, then the log entry includes name, namespace, and resourceKind with correct values — measured by unit tests across all 15 action files\n- Given the module controller reconciles any of its 8 module handlers and encounters an error, when the error is logged, then the entry includes name, namespace, and resourceKind — measured by unit test\n- Given a cluster-scoped resource is reconciled and encounters an error, when the error is logged, then the entry includes name and resourceKind and namespace is omitted or empty — measured by unit test confirming no spurious namespace value\n- The lint rule covering this binary's converted call sites runs in CI and fails on any log.Error call embedding name/namespace in message text or missing required structured fields\n- Before/after log output comparison for a sample reconciliation error confirms structured fields appear and the production log consumer stack ingests both formats without configuration changes (dev-cluster validation), with text-based alert rules identified and updated with SRE/Support\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (P0): message-embedded identity converted to structured KV pairs — manager-binary portion\n- HLR-2 (P0): DSCI key-name migration\n- HLR-3 (P0): service controller resourceKind\n- HLR-4 (P0): action file name/namespace/resourceKind across 4 tiers\n- HLR-6 (P0): module controller + 8 module handlers\n- HLR-7 (P1, bundled): DSC and component controller resourceKind — bundled with the P0 conversion because it is the same log.Error call sites; a split would audit the same files twice\n\nWait — the epic body should reference HLRs how? The strategy HLRs aren't numbered in the strategy; they're bullet points. I'll number them HLR-1..HLR-8 in order and explain the numbering in the summary (\"HLR-n refers to the n-th High Level Requirement bullet in the strategy, in order\"). That's standard.\n\nE002 body:\n\nTitle: Standardize reconciliation error logging in the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary\n\n## Description\nBrings the same structured logging convention to the cloudmanager binary shipped with the rhods-operator, which handles multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave). Its three controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) are built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation using the shared reconciler pattern and already receive name/namespace from controller-runtime's request logger; this epic converts message-embedded resource identity in error paths to structured key-value pairs and adds resourceKind. Includes the part of the CI lint/static-analysis enforcement covering this binary's converted call sites.\n\n## Scope\n- Audit log.Error() call sites in the three cloudmanager infrastructure controllers' reconciliation error paths\n- Convert call sites embedding resource name or namespace in message strings to structured KV pairs (name, namespace)\n- Add resourceKind as a structured field to all reconciliation error logs in these controllers (kind not implied by controller name)\n- Author and verify the part of the CI lint rule covering this binary's converted call sites\n- Unit tests asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given a cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the log entry includes name, namespace, and resourceKind as structured fields — measured by unit test asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output\n- Given a cloudmanager reconciliation error log that previously embedded resource identity in the message string, when the code is updated, then it uses structured key-value pairs — measured by the lint rule/static analysis flagging zero remaining embedded-identity log.Error calls in this binary\n- The lint rule covering this binary's converted call sites runs in CI and fails on non-conforming log.Error calls\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-1 (P0): message-embedded identity converted to structured KV pairs — cloudmanager-binary portion\n- HLR-5 (P0): cloudmanager controllers include name, namespace, resourceKind\n\nE003 body:\n\nTitle: Document and publish the structured reconciliation log field convention\n\n## Description\nAuthors the contribution guideline that specifies the structured log field convention (required fields name, namespace, resourceKind; camelCase naming rationale; the three-problem diagnosis: message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys, missing resourceKind), with before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs and a DSCI key-migration guide, and publishes the convention for external operator adoption (Cluster Observability Operator components such as Loki Operator and Tempo Operator) on a voluntary basis per platform policy (overlay 0008 — RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies).\n\n## Scope (content outline inside Scope)\n- Contribution guideline for new controller development specifying:\n  - required structured fields (name, namespace, resourceKind)\n  - camelCase naming rationale (alignment with controller-runtime conventions)\n  - the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys, missing resourceKind)\n  - before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs\n  - DSCI key-name migration guide (Request.Name/DSCInitialization → name/namespace) with before/after query examples for release notes, supporting a single-release transition\n- Publication of the convention as a specification for voluntary adoption by external operator teams, including filing tracking issues in the COO projects per the risk mitigation\n- Technically reviewed against the implemented conversion work in the rhods-operator manager and cloudmanager binaries (the guideline documents what was built)\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- The structured log field convention is documented in a contribution guideline specifying required fields (name, namespace, resourceKind), camelCase naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis\n- The guideline includes before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured KV pairs and a DSCI key-migration guide with before/after query examples suitable for release notes\n- The convention is published as a specification for external operator adoption, and tracking issues are filed in the COO (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) projects\n- The guideline is technically reviewed against the implemented manager- and cloudmanager-binary conversions (reviewer confirms documented examples match shipped behavior)\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- HLR-8 (P1): document the convention as a contribution guideline and publish for external operator adoption\n\nNote: docs-authoring epic — priority derived from strategy frontmatter priority Major → P1 (docs-authoring priority exception).\n\nFrontmatter:\nE001: epic_id, title, parent_strat, component=OpenShift AI Productization, team=ODH Operator team, type=Implementation, priority=P0, dependencies=[] (none), ai_signals.*\nE002: same, dependencies=[]\nE003: implementation_type=docs-authoring, priority=P1, dependencies=[RHAISTRAT-8010-E001, RHAISTRAT-8010-E002], ai_signals.*\n\nSignal files: RHAISTRAT-8010-E001-ai-signals.md, -E002-, -E003-.\n\nE001 ai_signals:\n- change_specificity: 1 — exact file paths and controller/action-file inventory enumerated in strategy (components/<name>/<name>_controller_actions.go, services/{auth,gateway,monitoring}, datasciencecluster, modules) with per-tier field requirements\n- pattern_precedent: 1 — modules_controller_actions_inject_env.go already emits structured fields; framework controllers already emit name/namespace via controller-runtime request logger\n- adapter_pattern: 0 — no adapter/plugin pattern involved; mechanical call-site conversion\n- existing_foundation: 1 — extends existing logr/controller-runtime structured logging throughout the manager binary\n- open_questions: 0 — remaining open questions (extra fields, alert inventory, cloudmanager timing) don't change this epic's approach; required fields fixed by P0 HLRs\n- external_dependency: 1 — no upstream/vendor coordination; controller-runtime logr already provides KV support\n- human_process_gates: 0 — no approval gate on the code change; SRE/Support coordination on alert-rule updates is engineering collaboration captured as an AC\n- repo_access: 1 — target repo (rhods-operator) is accessible and was analyzed for architecture context\n- architecture_claims: 1 — corroborated by .context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md: controller-runtime/logr usage, component and service controller inventory, DSCInitialization reconciler, action-file architecture. Context summarizes \"three controller tiers\" vs SME-verified five — granularity of the summary, not a contradiction.\n\nE002 ai_signals:\n- change_specificity: 1 — three named controllers (AWS/Azure/CoreWeave KubernetesEngine), shared reconciler pattern, known gap (resourceKind + embedded identity)\n- pattern_precedent: 1 — same conversion pattern as manager binary; shared reconciler already provides name/namespace\n- adapter_pattern: 0\n- existing_foundation: 1 — extends existing shared-reconciler/logr foundation in cloudmanager\n- open_questions: 0 — release-timing question (same vs subsequent release) is scheduling, not approach\n- external_dependency: 1 — none for the code change\n- human_process_gates: 0\n- repo_access: 1\n- architecture_claims: 1 — rhods-operator.md confirms the cloudmanager binary (cmd/cloudmanager) and the AWS/Azure/CoreWeave KubernetesEngine CRDs/controllers\n\nE003 ai_signals:\n- change_specificity: 1 — AC enumerates guideline content: required fields, camelCase rationale, three-problem diagnosis, before/after examples, migration guide\n- pattern_precedent: 0 — no existing in-repo logging-convention guideline to model; content derived from the implemented work\n- adapter_pattern: 0 — N/A\n- existing_foundation: 0 — new guideline document; grounded in the conversion work but authored from scratch\n- open_questions: 0 — the PM/Observability question about additional fields could extend the documented field set additively but does not change the documentation approach\n- external_dependency: 0 — publication targets external COO projects (tracking issues), but the deliverable is not gated on their response; adoption is voluntary\n- human_process_gates: -1 — guideline requires technical review sign-off against the implementation, and the field-set decision has a pending PM/Observability owner\n- repo_access: 1 — docs repo/contribution guideline location accessible\n- architecture_claims: 1 — content grounded in rhods-operator.md context (logr usage, controller architecture, cloudmanager scope)\n\nNow write everything. Order: summary body first, then frontmatter set, then epic files, then signals files, then batch-read, validator, then decompose_complete=true as the FINAL action.\n\nNote the pipeline-state constraint: \"After launching each wave of agents, your next Bash call MUST be wait-for-wave\" — I'm not launching agents, so N/A.\n\nLet me write the decomposition summary. Use Write tool for bodies, then frontmatter.py set.\n\nOne check on Rule 11 + validator: E003 (docs) must be blocked by every main-plan Implementation epic — E001, E002 both in dependencies. ✓. Priority inheritance: E003 is docs → exempt. E001/E002 P0 with no deps. ✓. No konflux/license/repo epics. ✓. No enforcement-titled epic. ✓ (lint work folded in; epic titles don't match enforcement regex — \"Standardize reconciliation error logging...\" — good).\n\ncritical_path_length: longest path = 2 (E001→E003). epic_count=3.\n\nLet me now write files. Working dir: must use absolute paths or cd. Bash calls reset cwd — the frontmatter.py commands in the prompt are relative. I'll run with cwd via absolute path: `cd /path && python3 scripts/frontmatter.py ...`? The note says cd in compound command can trigger permission prompt; prefer absolute paths. I'll use absolute paths for both script and target file.\n\nLet me write the summary body now.","signature":"cd7e58d322794abfba0381c713007438