ere the resource is cluster-scoped.\n- Author and verify the part of the CI lint rule (or static-analysis check) covering this binary's converted call sites.\n- Unit tests asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Given a cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the log entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields — measured by a unit test asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output.\n- Given a cloudmanager reconciliation error log that previously embedded resource identity in the message string, when the code is updated, then it uses structured key-value pairs instead — measured by the lint rule / static analysis reporting zero remaining embedded-identity `log.Error` calls in this binary.\n- The lint rule covering this binary's converted call sites runs in CI and fails on `log.Error` calls embedding name/namespace in message text or missing required structured fields.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0) — reconciliation error logs that embed resource name or namespace in message strings converted to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`): cloudmanager-binary portion.\n- HLR-5 (P0) — all cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields.\n\n## Notes\n\nRelease timing (same release as the manager-binary work vs. a subsequent release) is an open PM/ODH Operator team scheduling decision; it does not change this epic's scope. The log-consumer regression and alert-rule coordination for the platform is tracked with the manager-binary work; cloudmanager runs only on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave), so its log-consumer surface is narrower.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","s