g:\n42\t  - the required structured fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) and when each applies (including cluster-scoped resources, where `namespace` is omitted or empty);\n43\t  - the camelCase naming rationale, matching controller-runtime's existing structured log key conventions;\n44\t  - the three-problem diagnosis: message-embedded resource identity, non-standard DSCI key names (`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`), and the missing `resourceKind` field;\n45\t  - before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured key-value pairs;\n46\t  - a DSCI key-name migration guide with before/after Loki/CloudWatch query examples suitable for release notes, supporting a single-release transition away from the old field names.\n47\t- Publication of the convention as a specification for voluntary external operator adoption, including filing tracking issues in the COO (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) projects so adoption is discoverable, while acknowledging external adoption is best-effort per platform policy.\n48\t- Technical validation of the guideline against the implemented conversions in the rhods-operator manager and cloudmanager binaries, so the documented examples match shipped behavior.\n49\t\n50\t## Acceptance Criteria\n51\t\n52\t- The structured log field convention is documented in a contribution guideline specifying the required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys, missing `resourceKind`).\n53\t- The guideline includes before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured key-value pairs, and a DSCI key-migration guide with before/after query examples that can be reused in release notes.\n54\t- The convention is published as a specification for external operator adoption, and tracking issues are filed in the COO (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) projects.\n55\t- The guideline is technically reviewed against the implemented manager-binary and cloudmanager-binary log conversions — the reviewer confirms the documented field names, examples, and migration guidance match the shipped behavior.\n56\t\n57\t## HLR Traceability\n58\t\n59\t- HLR-8 (P1) — document the convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development and publish for external operator adoption.\n60\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e3b0b98c623d1ec","agent_id":"a23d7c700483192f4","session_id":"93f42fce-654c-40a2-8301-f2dce4eb00ac","uuid":"8f9ebdb0-1951-4cab-987f-d0b24ccde058","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:51:43.074Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-853d868d8b838cfd","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
