d vice versa; arrows point dependency → dependent per convention. No circular dependencies, no invalid references, no branch files. Both edges are Rule 11 (implementations → docs-authoring) edges — valid, and correctly not counted as priority-inheritance or serialization violations. The E001 ∥ E002 parallelism is correct under Rule 15: same repo but disjoint controller/action-file sets; no shared artifact forces sequencing. Critical path length 2 matches the S-size heuristic (1-2). Cross-check of epic content for uncaptured data/artifact dependencies: E003's \"technical validation of the guideline against the implemented conversions\" consumes E001/E002 outputs and the edges exist; no other epic content implies a missing edge. No konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or license-validation epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are vacuously satisfied.\n\n### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\nAll three epics share the (OpenShift AI Productization, ODH Operator team) tuple — verified against `.context/rhai-components.txt`, where \"OpenShift AI Productization\" is canonical and \"rhods-operator\" is indeed absent, so the mapping note is accurate. The E001/E002 split by binary is a valid seam (separate `cmd/` entrypoints, disjoint controller sets, different deployment targets), not a component/team violation. E001 sits above the ~2-week guideline (~1.25–1.5 sprints) but is shape (b): Health Warnings entry #1 records the estimate, the why-not-split rationale (one mechanical pass over one audited call-site inventory; per-tier split would audit files twice and fragment the lint surface), and a concrete conditional re-split trigger with named split boundaries — a defensible planning trade-off, not docked. The CI lint rule is correctly folded per surface into E001/E002 Scope/ACs rather than a standalone enforcement epic. No scope duplication between E001 and E002 (disjoint binaries/call sites; each owns its binary's lint coverage). No work-product type mixing — E003 (content authoring) is separated from the code epics.\n\n### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\nE001/E002 are Implementation epics producing concrete artifacts (converted call sites, unit tests, per-binary lint rule coverage). E003 is Implementation with `implementation_type: docs-authoring` and produces a published guideline plus COO tracking issues. No Investigation epics, and the summary justifies that disposition per open question (each fails the \"does the answer change which epics exist or what they do?\" test — scheduling/additive-field/inventory questions, all dispositioned in Health Warnings #2–#5). No `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are set anywhere, consistent with no gates; no epic lists an Investigation in `dependencies`.\n\n### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\nAll three epics are Implementation type and carry `ai_signals` (the type-appropriate field); `investigation_signals` is null everywhere, correct. Signal values are consistent with strategy content and the ai-signals rationales: E001/E002 `change_specificity: 1` justified by exact file inventories and named controllers; `pattern_precedent: 1` justified by the in-repo `modules_controller_actions_inject_env.go` precedent and framework request-logger fields; `open_questions: 0` defensible since the remaining open questions (PM field-set, SRE alert inventory, release timing, 75% figure) demonstrably do not change the implementation approach — they are additive or scheduling concerns; `architecture_claims: 1` is correctly calibrated — the strategy asserts component states (logr usage, controller inventory, cloudmanager binary) and the rationale cites `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`, which I verified corroborates controller-runtime/logr usage, the DSCInitialization CRD/reconciler, and cmd/cloudmanager with the AWS/Azure/CoreWeave engines. E003's differentiated profile (pattern_precedent 0, existing_foundation 0 for a from-scratch document; human_process_gates -1 for technical-review sign-off and the PM-owned field-set question) is well reasoned. No arithmetic or threshold checking performed, per instructions.\n\n### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\nEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy's own acceptance criteria, each with a measurement method (lint/static analysis, unit tests on test-logger output, codebase grep, reviewer confirmation). Rule-mandated ACs: E003 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review-against-implementation AC ✓; this is not a replacement epic (no rollback/feature-flag AC required) and there is no konflux chain. E001's log-consumer validation AC is a condition on what the epic itself builds and verifies (deploy test build, compare before/after output, confirm ingestion), not an externally-granted availability precondition, so it is a valid AC. The SRE/Support alert-rule coordination is framed as work performed within the AC. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — E002 defers by name (\"manager-binary work\") and E003 by binary name, both fine.\n\n### 7. Completeness (2/2)\nCore scope fully covered: every strategy acceptance criterion maps to an epic AC (KV conversion, DSCI migration, service-controller resourceKind, 15 action files across 4 tiers, cloudmanager controllers, cluster-scoped resources, guideline). All four strategy risks are carried forward (missed call sites → audit checklist + lint rule; log-consumer regression → E001 AC; DSCI query breakage → E003 migration guide with release-note query examples; COO adoption delay → E003 publication + tracking issues). All four open questions and the 75% assumption are dispositioned in Health Warnings rather than silently dropped. The NFRs are carried (backwards compatibility as E001 scope/AC; performance correctly requires no verification per the strategy). External-operator scope (publish convention, voluntary adoption per overlay 0008) is fully in E003, with the correct decision not to create a COO-team epic. Docs