{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a454c236a5cc3d42","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tscore: 14\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: Completeness\n9\t  description: 'E002 carries no backwards-compatibility/log-consumer validation AC\n10\t    of its own — its Notes defer platform log-consumer and alert-rule coordination\n11\t    to the manager-binary work, but cloudmanager consumers (e.g., CloudWatch on AWS\n12\t    EKS/Azure AKS/CoreWeave) differ from the manager binary consumers. Correction:\n13\t    add a before/after ingestion-validation AC (or an explicit in-row risk note) to\n14\t    E002 covering its own log-consumer surface.'\n15\t- severity: minor\n16\t  criterion: Completeness\n17\t  description: 'The strategy Supporting Documentation lists a Design doc to be created\n18\t    (three-problem diagnosis, before/after code examples, DSCI key-migration guide);\n19\t    its content is subsumed by E003 guideline scope but the disposition is implicit.\n20\t    Correction: record explicitly in the decomposition summary or E003 scope that\n21\t    the design-doc content is delivered as part of the E003 contribution guideline.'\n22\terror: null\n23\t---\n24\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-8010\n25\t\n26\t## Review Summary\n27\t\n28\tScore: 14/14 — pass\n29\tRecommendation: accept\n30\t\n31\tContract validator: OK (`skills/epic-decompose/scripts/validate_decomposition.py RHAISTRAT-8010` exited 0 — body sections, canonical component names, Rule 11 docs-last coverage, priority inheritance, and implementation-type parallelism all pass). Triage consistency: `triage: null` (full decomposition) with 3 epics — consistent, no contradiction. Two minor completeness notes recorded in `issues`; neither affects execution correctness.\n32\t\n33\t## Criterion Details\n34\t\n35\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n36\tAll 8 HLRs map to epics: HLR-1 → E001+E002 (split by binary, each converting its own call sites), HLR-2/3/4/6 → E001, HLR-5 → E002, HLR-7 → E001 (bundled), HLR-8 → E003. Traceability matrix present in the summary and per-epic HLR Traceability sections match it. Priority inheritance: E001/E002 (blocking E003) are P0; E003 is a `docs-authoring` epic at P1, which is the documented exemption (strategy Jira priority Major → P1). HLR-7 (P1) bundled into P0 epic E001 is compliant, not priority collapse: it is the same `log.Error` call sites as HLR-1/HLR-4 (adding `resourceKind` happens in the same edited lines), i.e., incidental to the P0 work rather than a distinct deferrable feature, and the bundling rationale is recorded inline in the matrix row and repeated in E001's HLR Traceability.\n37\t\n38\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n39\tDAG is E001 → E003, E002 → E003 with E001 ∥ E002 as roots. Every diagram edge has a matching frontmatter `dependencies` entry in the target epic (E003 depends on RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 and -E002) and vice versa; arrows point dependency → dependent per convention. No circular dependencies, no invalid references, no branch files. Both edges are Rule 11 (implementations → docs-authoring) edges — valid, and correctly not counted as priority-inheritance or serialization violations. The E001 ∥ E002 parallelism is correct under Rule 15: same repo but disjoint controller/action-file sets; no shared artifact forces sequencing. Critical path length 2 matches the S-size heuristic (1-2). Cross-check of epic content for uncaptured data/artifact dependencies: E003's \"technical validation of the guideline against the implemented conversions\" consumes E001/E002 outputs and the edges exist; no other epic content implies a missing edge. No konflux-onboarding, repo-onboarding, or license-validation epics exist, so Rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are vacuously satisfied.\n40\t\n41\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n42\tAll three epics share the (OpenShift AI Productization, ODH Operator team) tuple — verified against `.context/rhai-components.txt`, where \"OpenShift AI Productization\" is canonical and \"rhods-operator\" is indeed absent, so the mapping note is accurate. The E001/E002 split by binary is a valid seam (separate `cmd/` entrypoints, disjoint controller sets, different deployment targets), not a component/team violation. E001 sits above the ~2-week guideline (~1.25–1.5 sprints) but is shape (b): Health Warnings entry #1 records the estimate, the why-not-split rationale (one mechanical pass over one audited call-site inventory; per-tier split would audit files twice and fragment the lint surface), and a concrete conditional re-split trigger with named split boundaries — a defensible planning trade-off, not docked. The CI lint rule is correctly folded per surface into E001/E002 Scope/ACs rather than a standalone enforcement epic. No scope duplication between E001 and E002 (disjoint binaries/call sites; each owns its binary's lint coverage). No work-product type mixing — E003 (content authoring) is separated from the code epics.\n43\t\n44\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n45\tE001/E002 are Implementation epics producing concrete artifacts (converted call sites, unit tests, per-binary lint rule coverage). E003 is Implementation with `implementation_type: docs-authoring` and produces a published guideline plus COO tracking issues. No Investigation epics, and the summary justifies that disposition per open question (each fails the \"does the answer change which epics exist or what they do?\" test — scheduling/additive-field/inventory questions, all dispositioned in Health Warnings #2–#5). No `gated_by`/`gate_failure_impact` fields are set anywhere, consistent with no gates; no epic lists an Investigation in `dependencies`.\n46\t\n47\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n48\tAll three epics are Implementation type and carry `ai_signals` (the type-appropriate field); `investigation_signals` is null everywhere, correct. Signal values are consistent with strategy content and the ai-signals rationales: E001/E002 `change_specificity: 1` justified by exact file inventories and named controllers; `pattern_precedent: 1` justified by the in-repo `modules_controller_actions_inject_env.go` precedent and framework request-logger fields; `open_questions: 0` defensible since the remaining open questions (PM field-set, SRE alert inventory, release timing, 75% figure) demonstrably do not change the implementation approach — they are additive or scheduling concerns; `architecture_claims: 1` is correctly calibrated — the strategy asserts component states (logr usage, controller inventory, cloudmanager binary) and the rationale cites `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md`, which I verified corroborates controller-runtime/logr usage, the DSCInitialization CRD/reconciler, and cmd/cloudmanager with the AWS/Azure/CoreWeave engines. E003's differentiated profile (pattern_precedent 0, existing_foundation 0 for a from-scratch document; human_process_gates -1 for technical-review sign-off and the PM-owned field-set question) is well reasoned. No arithmetic or threshold checking performed, per instructions.\n49\t\n50\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n51\tEvery epic has testable ACs derived from the strategy's own acceptance criteria, each with a measurement method (lint/static analysis, unit tests on test-logger output, codebase grep, reviewer confirmation). Rule-mandated ACs: E003 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review-against-implementation AC ✓; this is not a replacement epic (no rollback/feature-flag AC required) and there is no konflux chain. E001's log-consumer validation AC is a condition on what the epic itself builds and verifies (deploy test build, compare before/after output, confirm ingestion), not an externally-granted availability precondition, so it is a valid AC. The SRE/Support alert-rule coordination is framed as work performed within the AC. No epic body references sibling epics by draft ID — E002 defers by name (\"manager-binary work\") and E003 by binary name, both fine.\n52\t\n53\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n54\tCore scope fully covered: every strategy acceptance criterion maps to an epic AC (KV conversion, DSCI migration, service-controller resourceKind, 15 action files across 4 tiers, cloudmanager controllers, cluster-scoped resources, guideline). All four strategy risks are carried forward (missed call sites → audit checklist + lint rule; log-consumer regression → E001 AC; DSCI query breakage → E003 migration guide with release-note query examples; COO adoption delay → E003 publication + tracking issues). All four open questions and the 75% assumption are dispositioned in Health Warnings rather than silently dropped. The NFRs are carried (backwards compatibility as E001 scope/AC; performance correctly requires no verification per the strategy). External-operator scope (publish convention, voluntary adoption per overlay 0008) is fully in E003, with the correct decision not to create a COO-team epic. Docs-authoring: the strategy's Documentation Support gate is \"Pending human review\" (not Yes), and a docs-authoring epic exists anyway via HLR-8. Two minor notes: (a) E002's Notes defer platform log-consumer/alert coordination to the manager-binary work, but cloudmanager's consumers (e.g., CloudWatch on EKS/AKS/CoreWeave) differ from the manager's — E002 carries no backwards-compat validation AC of its own; (b) the strategy's \"Design doc: To be created\" (three-problem diagnosis, before/after examples, DSCI migration guide) is content-subsumed by E003's guideline but that disposition is implicit rather than recorded. Neither is a scope gap in a primary capability.\n55\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a7335c129edb3f57","agent_id":"aa0fa9c21920d82b5","session_id":"93f42fce-654c-40a2-8301-f2dce4eb00ac","uuid":"1a191707-aa24-47c9-8da2-73412e672757","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:53:43.061Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-to