{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4601de882f9d8bd","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n3\tepic_count: 3\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-8010\n11\t\n12\tOperator reconciliation log standardization with structured resource context (rhods-operator, manager + cloudmanager binaries).\n13\t\n14\t## Triage Outcome\n15\t\n16\tNot below-threshold and not docs-only — full decomposition.\n17\t\n18\t- **Below-threshold escape — multiple distinct work streams:** although the strategy is S-sized, affects a single component (rhods-operator) worked by a single team (ODH Operator team), and the code conversion is highly AI-implementable, the scope spans two distinct work streams: (1) mechanical code implementation across two binaries, and (2) substantive documentation authoring — a new contribution guideline (required fields, camelCase rationale, three-problem diagnosis, before/after examples, DSCI migration guide) published for external operator adoption, with its own dedicated P1 HLR and ~0.5 sprint of the estimate. This is not an incidental doc touch-up, so the below-threshold path does not apply.\n19\t- **Not docs-only:** rhods-operator requires code changes.\n20\t\n21\tHLR numbering below refers to the High Level Requirements bullets of the strategy in order (HLR-1 … HLR-8).\n22\t\n23\t## Epic List\n24\t\n25\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n26\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E001 | Standardize reconciliation error logging in the rhods-operator manager binary | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n28\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E002 | Standardize reconciliation error logging in the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary | Implementation | ODH Operator team | P0 |\n29\t| RHAISTRAT-8010-E003 | Document and publish the structured reconciliation log field convention | Implementation (docs-authoring) | ODH Operator team | P1 |\n30\t\n31\t## Dependency DAG\n32\t\n33\t```mermaid\n34\tgraph TD\n35\t    E001[\"E001 manager binary conversion (P0)\"] --> E003[\"E003 convention guideline + publication (P1)\"]\n36\t    E002[\"E002 cloudmanager binary conversion (P0)\"] --> E003\n37\t```\n38\t\n39\tE001 and E002 are roots and run in parallel.\n40\t\n41\t## DAG Justification\n42\t\n43\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n44\t|------|------|-----------|\n45\t| E001 ∥ E002 (no edge) | Rule 15 | Same repo, different areas: the manager and cloudmanager binaries have disjoint controller/action-file sets. Merge conflicts on shared test/lint wiring are a coordination risk, not a dependency. Each binary's conversion is independently complete and valuable. |\n46\t| E001 → E003 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic is blocked by all Implementation epics; the guideline documents the convention as implemented in the manager binary. |\n47\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 11 | docs-authoring epic is blocked by all Implementation epics; the guideline documents the convention as implemented in the cloudmanager binary. |\n48\t\n49\tNo Investigation epics: every open question in the strategy was evaluated against \"does the answer change which epics exist or what they do?\" and none qualifies (see Health Warnings for the disposition of each).\n50\t\n51\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n52\t\n53\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n54\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n55\t| HLR-1 — convert message-embedded identity to structured KV (`name`, `namespace`) | P0 | E001, E002 | Spans both binaries; each epic converts its own call sites. |\n56\t| HLR-2 — DSCI migrated from `Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization` to camelCase | P0 | E001 | DSCI controller is manager-binary scope. |\n57\t| HLR-3 — service controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P0 | E001 | |\n58\t| HLR-4 — action file error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (15 files, 4 tiers) | P0 | E001 | All 15 action files (8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module) are manager-binary scope. |\n59\t| HLR-5 — cloudmanager controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` | P0 | E002 | |\n60\t| HLR-6 — module controller error logs include `name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind` (8 handlers) | P0 | E001 | |\n61\t| HLR-7 — DSC and component controller error logs include `resourceKind` | P1 | E001 (bundled) | Bundled: same `log.Error` call sites as HLR-1/HLR-4 in the DSC and component controllers and their action files — adding `resourceKind` happens in the same edited lines as the KV conversion; a split audits and re-reviews the same files twice. Not separately schedulable. |\n62\t| HLR-8 — document convention as contribution guideline and publish for external adoption | P1 | E003 | |\n63\t\n64\tFull coverage: HLR-1 through HLR-8 each map to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n65\t\n66\t## Epic Boundary Reasoning\n67\t\n68\t- **One epic per binary (E001 manager, E002 cloudmanager):** the manager and cloudmanager binaries are architecturally distinct sub-systems (separate `cmd/` entrypoints, disjoint controller sets, different deployment targets — OpenShift vs AWS EKS / Azure AKS / CoreWeave) warranting separate PR review cycles. Within each binary the conversion is one logical change for one team, so no further split by controller tier.\n69\t- **CI lint rule folded per surface, not a standalone epic:** the strategy's CI lint rule / static-analysis check is a shared-surface enforcement deliverable spanning both binaries' converted call sites. Each binary's epic authors and verifies the part of the lint rule covering its own binary's converted call sites (Scope + verification AC in E001 and E002). No standalone \"CI lint rule\" epic.\n70\t- **Docs as a separate work-product epic (E003):** the contribution guideline is substantive content authoring (new doc topic with before/after examples and a migration guide), a different work-product type with a different review cycle than the code conversion. `implementation_type: docs-authoring`; priority P1 per the docs-authoring exception (strategy frontmatter `priority: Major` → P1); it does not push priority upstream.\n71\t\n72\t## Health Warnings\n73\t\n74\t1. **Epic size — E001 above the ~2-week guideline, kept whole.** E001 is ~1.25–1.5 sprints by the strategy's effort arithmetic (~0.5 sprint audit + ~0.75–1 sprint manager conversion). Kept whole because the conversion is one mechanical pass over a single audited call-site inventory in one binary, and a per-tier split would audit the same files twice and fragment the lint-rule surface. **Re-split trigger:** the manager-binary call-site audit exceeds the strategy's 75%-of-calls estimate by enough to push the epic past 2 sprints — then split along controller-tier boundaries (DSC + component controllers + component action files / module controller + module action files / DSCI + service controllers + service action files), each fragment carrying the part of the lint rule covering its files.\n75\t2. **Open question — additional fields (owner: PM / Observability team):** whether to add fields beyond `name`/`namespace`/`resourceKind` (reconciliation attempt count, error category). Does not change epic structure — required fields are fixed by the P0 HLRs; any addition is additive follow-up on the same call sites (would extend E001/E002 scope and the E003 guideline). Captured as a note, not an Investigation.\n76\t3. **Open question — alert-rule inventory (owner: SRE / Support):** which text-based alerts rely on message-string patterns. Resolution is the pre-merge log-consumer regression validation, captured as an AC and risk on both code epics — implementation proceeds the same way regardless; failure = fix-and-retry.\n77\t4. **Open question — cloudmanager release timing (owner: PM / ODH Operator team):** same release as manager or a subsequent one. A scheduling decision on E002 only; both code epics are P0 per their HLRs and the decomposition is unchanged either way.\n78\t5. **Assumption to validate in-epic:** the 75% embedded-identity figure is SME-sourced and needs codebase-audit confirmation — the audit is the first step of each code epic; a different percentage changes effort, not structure.\n79\t6. **Priority note:** HLR-7 (P1) is bundled into P0 epic E001 — see the HLR Traceability Matrix row for the inline rationale.\n80\t\n81\t## Tiered Delivery\n82\t\n83\tNot applicable. The only external dependency with uncertain timing is COO team adoption of the published convention (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator). No RHOAI work is gated on it — the deliverable is the published convention itself, and adoption is voluntary per platform policy (overlay 0008). Both code epics deliver full independent value regardless of COO timing, so there is no Tier 1/Tier 2 split.\n84\t\n85\t## Component and Team Notes\n86\t\n87\t- **Canonical component mapping (non-obvious):** the strategy's component \"rhods-operator\" is not in `.context/rhai-components.txt`. All three epics use the closest canonical name **\"OpenShift AI Productization\"** (the RHOAI platform-operator productization work). `submit.py` strips unknown component names, so this mapping is required; if a dedicated operator component is later added to the canonical list, re-tag.\n88\t- **COO team (Cluster Observability Operator — Loki Operator, Tempo Operator):** passive recipient, no code changes and no validation work owned by them. Publishing the convention (and filing tracking issues in the COO projects, per the strategy's risk mitigation) is scope of E003. No separate COO-team epic and no upstream Implementation epic — per platform policy overlay 0008, RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies, and the RFE approval comment confirms voluntary adoption.\n89\t- **Staff Engineer / SME input overrides logged (Step 1.5):** the SME-corrected controller architecture (5 tiers; 15 action files: 8 component + 3 service + 1 DSC + 3 module; module controller with 8 module handlers) is used throughout, overriding the AI-generated \"~16 component action files\" figure. The strategy's original open question about component action file counts was answered by the SME (8/8 components have exactly one) and is not carried forward as an unknown. camelCase field naming, the shared-reconciler fact for cloudmanager, and the cluster-scoped-resource AC are SME confirmations reflected in epic scope.\n90\t- **Architecture claims corroborated:** `.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.6-ea.1/rhods-operator.md` confirms controller-runtime/logr usage throughout the controller hierarchy, the component/service controller inventory, the DSCInitialization reconciler, and the cloudmanager binary (cmd/cloudmanager) with the AWS/Azure/CoreWeave KubernetesEngine CRDs. The context file's summary describes \"three controller tiers\" where the SME-verified count is five — a granularity difference in the context summary, not a contradiction (the context's integration tables list the DSCInitialization reconciler and the module/component handler watches).\n91\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-93d3e169f0e77b89","agent_id":"ad6c0b5880d4b7482","session_id":"93f42fce-654c-40a2-8301-f2dce4eb00ac","uuid":"75197a1a-4476-43de-9691-03ec30d651e9","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:54:54.035Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a4601de882f9d8bd","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
