{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b43d8a2823c75487","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n3\ttitle: Standardize reconciliation error logging in the rhods-operator cloudmanager\n4\t  binary\n5\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n6\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n7\tteam: ODH Operator team\n8\ttype: Implementation\n9\tpriority: P0\n10\tai_signals:\n11\t  change_specificity: 1\n12\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n13\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n14\t  existing_foundation: 1\n15\t  open_questions: 0\n16\t  external_dependency: 1\n17\t  human_process_gates: 0\n18\t  repo_access: 1\n19\t  architecture_claims: 1\n20\timplementation_type: null\n21\tdependencies: []\n22\tinvestigation_signals: null\n23\tai_implementability: High\n24\tai_implementability_score: 6\n25\tjira_key: null\n26\tbranch: null\n27\tgated_by: null\n28\tgate_failure_impact: null\n29\t---\n30\t# Standardize reconciliation error logging in the rhods-operator cloudmanager binary\n31\t\n32\t## Description\n33\t\n34\tApplies the same structured logging convention to the cloudmanager binary shipped with the rhods-operator, which handles multi-cloud infrastructure dependencies on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave). Its three reconciliation controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) are built on the same controller-runtime/logr foundation using the shared reconciler pattern and already receive `name`/`namespace` from controller-runtime's request logger. This epic converts message-embedded resource identity in error paths to structured key-value pairs, adds the `resourceKind` structured field, and includes authoring and verifying the part of the CI lint/static-analysis enforcement that covers this binary's converted call sites.\n35\t\n36\t## Scope\n37\t\n38\t- Audit `log.Error()` call sites in the reconciliation error paths of the three cloudmanager infrastructure controllers (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine).\n39\t- For each call site embedding resource name or namespace in the message string, extract the identity fields and convert to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`), removing the redundant identity from the message text.\n40\t- Add `resourceKind` as a structured field to all reconciliation error logs in these controllers (kind is not implied by the controller name).\n41\t- Handle cluster-scoped infrastructure resources consistently with the platform convention: emit `name` and `resourceKind`, with `namespace` omitted or empty where the resource is cluster-scoped.\n42\t- Author and verify the part of the CI lint rule (or static-analysis check) covering this binary's converted call sites.\n43\t- Unit tests asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output.\n44\t- Backwards-compatibility validation for this binary's own log-consumer surface: before/after log output comparison for a sample cloudmanager reconciliation error, confirming the log consumers used on the cloudmanager deployment targets (e.g., CloudWatch on AWS EKS, and the equivalent native log ingestion on Azure AKS and CoreWeave) ingest both formats without configuration changes.\n45\t\n46\t## Acceptance Criteria\n47\t\n48\t- Given a cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) encounters a reconciliation error, when the error is logged, then the log entry includes `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields — measured by a unit test asserting field presence and correctness in test logger output.\n49\t- Given a cloudmanager reconciliation error log that previously embedded resource identity in the message string, when the code is updated, then it uses structured key-value pairs instead — measured by the lint rule / static analysis reporting zero remaining embedded-identity `log.Error` calls in this binary.\n50\t- The lint rule covering this binary's converted call sites runs in CI and fails on `log.Error` calls embedding name/namespace in message text or missing required structured fields.\n51\t- Before/after log output comparison for a sample cloudmanager reconciliation error confirms the structured fields appear and the log consumers on the cloudmanager deployment targets (e.g., CloudWatch on AWS EKS, and the equivalent native log ingestion on Azure AKS and CoreWeave) ingest both formats without configuration changes.\n52\t\n53\t## HLR Traceability\n54\t\n55\t- HLR-1 (P0) — reconciliation error logs that embed resource name or namespace in message strings converted to structured key-value pairs (`name`, `namespace`): cloudmanager-binary portion.\n56\t- HLR-5 (P0) — all cloudmanager controller (AWSKubernetesEngine, AzureKubernetesEngine, CoreWeaveKubernetesEngine) reconciliation error logs include `name`, `namespace`, and `resourceKind` as structured fields.\n57\t\n58\t## Notes\n59\t\n60\tRelease timing (same release as the manager-binary work vs. a subsequent release) is an open PM/ODH Operator team scheduling decision; it does not change this epic's scope. Platform log-consumer regression (Loki on OpenShift) and platform alert-rule coordination are tracked with the manager-binary work; this epic validates its own log-consumer surface — cloudmanager runs only on non-OpenShift Kubernetes (AWS EKS, Azure AKS, CoreWeave), whose consumers (e.g., CloudWatch) differ from the manager binary's, which is why the ingestion-validation AC above is scoped to this epic rather than deferred.\n61\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-93d3e169f0e77b89","agent_id":"ad6c0b5880d4b7482","session_id":"93f42fce-654c-40a2-8301-f2dce4eb00ac","uuid":"a324d213-42da-4732-bd31-350a9583492c","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:54:57.886Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-b43d8a2823c75487","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
