{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a18677b1e95c19e4","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-8010-E003\n3\ttitle: Document and publish the structured reconciliation log field convention\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8010\n5\tcomponent: OpenShift AI Productization\n6\tteam: ODH Operator team\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\timplementation_type: docs-authoring\n10\tdependencies:\n11\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E001\n12\t- RHAISTRAT-8010-E002\n13\tai_signals:\n14\t  change_specificity: 1\n15\t  pattern_precedent: 0\n16\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n17\t  existing_foundation: 0\n18\t  open_questions: 0\n19\t  external_dependency: 0\n20\t  human_process_gates: -1\n21\t  repo_access: 1\n22\t  architecture_claims: 1\n23\tinvestigation_signals: null\n24\tai_implementability: Medium\n25\tai_implementability_score: 2\n26\tjira_key: null\n27\tbranch: null\n28\tgated_by: null\n29\tgate_failure_impact: null\n30\t---\n31\t# Document and publish the structured reconciliation log field convention\n32\t\n33\t## Description\n34\t\n35\tAuthors the contribution guideline that specifies the structured log field convention adopted for rhods-operator reconciliation error logs — required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale (alignment with controller-runtime's structured log key conventions), and the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys, missing `resourceKind`) — with before/after code examples and a DSCI key-migration guide, and publishes the convention for voluntary adoption by external operator teams (Cluster Observability Operator components: Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) per platform policy overlay 0008 (RHOAI does not install, manage, or control external operator dependencies).\n36\t\n37\t## Scope\n38\t\n39\tContent outline of the guideline and publication deliverables:\n40\t\n41\t- Contribution guideline for new controller development specifying:\n42\t  - the required structured fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`) and when each applies (including cluster-scoped resources, where `namespace` is omitted or empty);\n43\t  - the camelCase naming rationale, matching controller-runtime's existing structured log key conventions;\n44\t  - the three-problem diagnosis: message-embedded resource identity, non-standard DSCI key names (`Request.Name`/`DSCInitialization`), and the missing `resourceKind` field;\n45\t  - before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured key-value pairs;\n46\t  - a DSCI key-name migration guide with before/after Loki/CloudWatch query examples suitable for release notes, supporting a single-release transition away from the old field names.\n47\t- Publication of the convention as a specification for voluntary external operator adoption, including filing tracking issues in the COO (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) projects so adoption is discoverable, while acknowledging external adoption is best-effort per platform policy.\n48\t- Technical validation of the guideline against the implemented conversions in the rhods-operator manager and cloudmanager binaries, so the documented examples match shipped behavior.\n49\t- Disposition of the strategy's planned design doc (Supporting Documentation: \"Design doc: to be created\" — three-problem diagnosis, before/after code examples, DSCI key-migration guide): that content is delivered as sections of this contribution guideline rather than as a separate design-doc deliverable; no standalone design doc is produced.\n50\t\n51\t## Acceptance Criteria\n52\t\n53\t- The structured log field convention is documented in a contribution guideline specifying the required fields (`name`, `namespace`, `resourceKind`), the camelCase naming rationale, and the three-problem diagnosis (message-embedded identity, non-standard DSCI keys, missing `resourceKind`).\n54\t- The guideline includes before/after code examples for converting message-embedded identity to structured key-value pairs, and a DSCI key-migration guide with before/after query examples that can be reused in release notes.\n55\t- The convention is published as a specification for external operator adoption, and tracking issues are filed in the COO (Loki Operator, Tempo Operator) projects.\n56\t- The guideline is technically reviewed against the implemented manager-binary and cloudmanager-binary log conversions — the reviewer confirms the documented field names, examples, and migration guidance match the shipped behavior.\n57\t\n58\t## HLR Traceability\n59\t\n60\t- HLR-8 (P1) — document the convention as a contribution guideline for new controller development and publish for external operator adoption.\n61\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-93d3e169f0e77b89","agent_id":"ad6c0b5880d4b7482","session_id":"93f42fce-654c-40a2-8301-f2dce4eb00ac","uuid":"e0363373-aff6-4f5b-b61d-170c1a7fd4ac","timestamp":"2026-10-26T21:54:58.490Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-a18677b1e95c19e4","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-8010 decomposition"}
